# EPOCHREALTIME precision: bash vs zsh
Q: is the fractional part the same width in both shells?

bash 5.3.20 : value=1795443647.561259  fraction_digits=6
zsh  5.9.2  : value=1795443647.5582363605  fraction_digits=10
  (zsh needs `zmodload zsh/datetime`; without the module $EPOCHREALTIME is
   empty, and ${#...} on it is 0 -- that is a trap for any adapter.)

CONCLUSION: NO. bash gives 6 digits (microseconds); this zsh gives 10 digits
(nanoseconds). Consequences for an adapter:
  1. `$(( (${b/./} - ${a/./}) / 1000 ))` overflows signed 64-bit in zsh: a
     10-digit seconds value concatenated with a 10-digit fraction is a
     20-digit integer. zsh then truncates with a warning or returns garbage;
     during development a 0 ms command was reported as 115 ms.
  2. Formatting ts_wall from the raw fraction yields
     '2026-11-23T22:19:29.3777232170Z', which is not ISO-8601.
The zsh adapter truncates the fraction to 6 digits for arithmetic and for
formatting; bash needs no truncation.
